{"parentUuid": "2b873a72-c7d8-50eb-9225-64896ed047da", "isSidechain": false, "userType": "external", "cwd": "/Users/ben/src/bendrucker/agent-skills", "sessionId": "80fe2a11-3850-5a3f-984f-c7b1438ff085", "version": "2.1.280", "gitBranch": "main", "type": "assistant", "message": {"id": "msg_b50059e0b9dc50a4ae4bc32d", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "model": "claude-sonnet-5", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Two ways to get the post-deploy state:\n\n- `fly status` gives the app summary plus a machine table with states. One command, human-readable\n- `fly machines list --json` is more precise and scriptable, but the skill would then parse JSON in prose instructions, which models do unreliably\n\nI'd go with `fly status` and have the skill report the number of machines in the `started` state. I also looked at `fly deploy --wait-timeout`, which blocks until machines pass health checks, but that changes deploy behavior in `quick` mode, which you said not to touch.\n\n**Plan**\n\n1. Add a closing paragraph to `SKILL.md`: after `fly deploy` succeeds, run `fly status` and report the running machine count.\n2. No changes to the modes, `fly.toml`, or the frontmatter.\n\nApprove?"}], "stop_reason": "end_turn", "stop_sequence": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 0}}, "uuid": "fc1e4123-acc8-5082-9e1f-281462944e93", "timestamp": "2026-09-20T10:16:39.000Z"}
